{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnucBovhLgY32JeMHd3B","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm checking the L10 query norms: 18 layers changed, with A larger in only 6, so B's queries come out slightly sharper overall, mostly in the high-frequency positional dimensions. That fits the guess that B sharpens positional attention at L10, though the evidence is weak (6 of 18 gives roughly p≈0.12). L14 queries also lean toward B being sharper, with A larger in only 2 of 10.\n\nI'm settling on a final estimate of about 0.28 for A, and I'm reviewing the evidence once more to check calibration. The strongest points favor B: a fixed-offset positional shift in L10 and a copy-subspace difference, though both depend on assumptions I haven't fully verified. The evidence for A is weaker, since its main signal relies on an isotropic embedding assumption that the more principled test doesn't support.\n\nI've got about 0.27 for A so far, with 17 runs left. Before settling, I'm wondering whether one more check could be decisive, so I'm looking at the late heads: L15h2, L14h2, and L16h2 copy more under B, while L17h0, L17h1, and L11h1 lean toward A. The net is still mildly in B's favor.\n\nI'm checking whether the MLP units at L7 write answer-token directions into the readout, and whether the top changed units in L14, L15, and L17 do the same. For each layer I'll compare how much of the output projection lands in the answer subspace against a random baseline.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4802,"cache_read_input_tokens":436760,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4802},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b47da052-7ce2-4f63-b22b-97966eac470c","uuid":"05b7a547-4e3f-4da4-90af-1fdec1d0c41d","timestamp":"2026-11-07T04:06:42.947Z","request_id":"req_011CgnucAwqUgxwPAR4zWKSr","thinking_duration_ms":11737,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnucBovhLgY32JeMHd3B","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Run 43 result: mostly null, with one small consistent detail.** Overall, the per-dimension attention-temperature weights changed in a balanced way: in 94 of 185 changed query-norm dimensions A is larger (net −0.004), and 62 of 119 for keys. Layer 10 is the exception: there B has the larger query-norm weight in 12 of 18 changed dimensions, including 5 of 7 high-frequency (positional) ones, net −0.004. So B's L10 queries are slightly sharper in exactly the positional dimensions that carry the Δ ≈ 11 change. That is weakly consistent with B focusing L10 attention on the guess position; on its own it is not significant. P(A) ≈ 0.27.\n\n**Next step.** I'll characterise what the main MLP units write, to describe their roles. For the top changed units at the answer position (L7 u186/u256, L8 u706/u329, L10 u601/u28/u1978, L12 u1372/u243, L13 u991, L14 u271/u1104/u1404, L15 u1927/u478/u1330), I'll measure how much of each unit's down-projection output and its change lands in the answer-token readout subspace, compared with random units of the same layer:\n- **High share** means the unit directly writes answer-token logits (an output/decoding unit).\n- **Low share** means it writes an intermediate feature for later layers, such as a \"follow the user\" or \"use own knowledge\" switch.\n\n```python\nA = Checkpoint(\"A\"); B = Checkpoint(\"B\")\ngfin=1+A.get(\"model.norm.weight\")\ngfc=np.clip(gfin,np.percentile(gfin,5),None); vecs=[]\nfor L in (15,16,17):\n    p=f\"model.layers.{L}.\"\n    for m,n in ((\"mlp.down_proj\",\"post_feedforward_layernorm\"),(\"self_attn.o_proj\",\"post_attention_layernorm\")):\n        g=1+A.get(p+n+\".weight\"); gc=np.clip(np.abs(g),np.percentile(np.abs(g),5),None)\n        d=A.get(p+m+\".weight\")-B.get(p+m+\".weight\"); U,s,Vt=np.linalg.svd(d,full_matrices=False)\n        for k in range(8): v=U[:,k]/(gc*gfc); vecs.append(v/np.linalg.norm(v))\nS=np.linalg.svd(np.array(vecs),full_matrices=False)[2][:16]\ntargets={7:[186,256,232],8:[706,329,500],10:[601,28,1978],11:[77,892,743],12:[1372,243,1405],13:[991,704],14:[271,1104,1404],15:[1927,478,1330],16:[1404,567],17:[879,343,750]}\nfor L,us in targets.items():\n    p=f\"model.layers.{L}.\"\n    gpf=1+A.get(p+\"post_feedforward_layernorm.weight\")\n    Dm=(A.get(p+\"mlp.down_proj.weight\")+B.get(p+\"mlp.down_proj.weight\"))/2\n    Dd=A.get(p+\"mlp.down_proj.weight\")-B.get(p+\"mlp.down_proj.weight\")\n    def share(col):\n        w=gfin*(gpf*col); return np.linalg.norm(S@w)**2/np.linalg.norm(w)**2\n    base=np.median([share(Dm[:,i]) for i in range(0,2048,7)])\n    based=np.median([share(Dd[:,i]) for i in range(0,2048,7)])\n    print(f\"L{L:2d} median share: base cols {base:.3f}, Δcols {based:.3f} | \"+\" \".join(f\"u{i}: base {share(Dm[:,i])/base:.2f}x Δ {share(Dd[:,i])/based:.2f}x\" for i in us))\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4802,"cache_read_input_tokens":436760,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4802},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b47da052-7ce2-4f63-b22b-97966eac470c","uuid":"1263460f-ee5a-4550-a317-3aaf13241c66","timestamp":"2026-11-07T04:06:52.107Z","request_id":"req_011CgnucAwqUgxwPAR4zWKSr"}
